' =====================================================================
'  Annotated class diagram — self-contained (single file, no !include).
'
'  Scope: the pibt/ package — Priority Inheritance with Backtracking
'  (Okumura et al., 2022), core/'s only dependent besides log/. This
'  documents code that already exists and runs — python -m
'  pibt.demo_one_shot for the one-shot regime, python -m
'  pibt.demo_lifelong for the rotating-goal one — catching the diagram
'  up to the package, not the other way round. core/ classes it
'  touches (PathPlanner, MissionOrchestrator, Agent, Grid2D,
'  Coordinates2D, Orientation, StepOutcome, AssignmentManifest,
'  Objective) are shown as stubs here; see
'  Class_Diagram/class_diag_commented.puml for their real detail —
'  this file does not repeat it.
'
'  Regenerate with:
'      plantuml diagrammes/Class_Diagram/Pibt/class_diag_commented.puml
'
'  Output:
'      pibt_class_diagram_commente.png
' =====================================================================

@startuml pibt_class_diagram_commente
title Simulation — pibt/ class diagram (annotated: context & design patterns)

skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontSize 13
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam ClassBorderColor #4A90D9
skinparam ClassBackgroundColor #EAF4FF
skinparam ClassHeaderBackgroundColor #4A90D9
skinparam ClassHeaderFontColor #FFFFFF
skinparam PackageBorderColor #888888
skinparam PackageBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C9B458
skinparam linetype ortho

' =====================================================================
'  core — stubs only; this package's real home is Class_Diagram/
' =====================================================================
package "core (stubs)" #EAF4FF {
    abstract class PathPlanner
    abstract class MissionOrchestrator
    class Agent
    class Grid2D
    class Coordinates2D
    class Orientation
    class StepOutcome
    class AssignmentManifest
    class Objective
}

' =====================================================================
'  pibt — Priority Inheritance with Backtracking
' =====================================================================
package "pibt" #E8F5E9 {

    enum GoalReachedPolicy {
        TRANSIENT
        PERMANENT
    }
    note bottom of GoalReachedPolicy
      **Whether the goal-reached demotion survives departure**
      An agent standing on its goal is demoted to base priority -inf
      so it stops contesting cells it does not need. TRANSIENT (the
      default) undoes that the instant the agent leaves its goal —
      mandatory once goals ever move again (a rotating-goal regime).
      PERMANENT never undoes it — measurably better when goals are
      fixed for the whole run (OneShotGoalDispatcher). Threaded in at
      construction because only the caller that owns the goal regime
      knows which is safe.
    end note

    class ReservationTable {
        - _reserved : dict[Coordinates2D, Agent]
        --
        + reserve(agent: Agent, pos: Coordinates2D) : void
        + release(agent: Agent) : void
        + get_reserving_agent(pos: Coordinates2D) : Agent | None
        + get_reservation(agent: Agent) : Coordinates2D | None
        + clear() : void
    }

    class PriorityManager {
        - _base    : dict[Agent, float]
        - _dynamic : dict[Agent, float]
        --
        + update(agents: list[Agent]) : void
        + get_priority(agent: Agent) : float
        + get_base(agent: Agent) : float
        + set_base(agent: Agent, p: float) : void
    }
    note bottom of PriorityManager
      **Base vs. dynamic, and why drift survives a rewrite**
      Base priorities are set explicitly (construction, inheritance,
      goal-reached demotion); dynamic priorities decay by 1 every
      update() to prevent starvation. set_base() carries the
      accumulated drift (dynamic - base) over rather than flattening
      it, so an agent handed a new base does not lose waiting credit
      it already built up.
    end note

    class "pibt_core" as PibtCore <<module>> {
        + {static} ORIENTATION_BY_DELTA : dict[Coordinates2D, Orientation]
        --
        + {static} run_pibt_pass(grid, goals, priorities, reservations, order, processed=None) : tuple
    }
    note bottom of PibtCore
      **Algorithm 1, extracted to be shared**
      The recursive core, re-parameterized to read an externally-owned
      ReservationTable/PriorityManager instead of `self` — so a future
      constrained caller (e.g. LaCAM's low level) can pre-seed one
      agent's reservation and let the rest cascade around it.
      run_pibt_pass() does not clear reservations itself: the caller
      decides what is already reserved before the pass starts.
      ORIENTATION_BY_DELTA is the one place in the repository that
      fixes an x/y <-> compass convention (x East, y South) — see the
      note on PIBTPlanner.orientation below.
    end note

    class PIBTPlanner {
        + goals : dict[Agent, Coordinates2D]
        - _reservations        : ReservationTable
        - _priorities          : PriorityManager
        - _goal_reached_policy : GoalReachedPolicy
        - _emit_orientation    : bool
        --
        + plan(agents: list[Agent], grid: Grid2D, intent: AssignmentManifest) : StepOutcome
        - _apply_intent(agents: list[Agent], intent: AssignmentManifest) : void
    }
    note top of PIBTPlanner
      **A thin per-tick wrapper around the shared core**
      Goal/priority bookkeeping plus one unconstrained call into
      run_pibt_pass(). Stateless about allocation — goals and base
      priorities are read from AssignmentManifest.goals (a list of
      Objectives, each already attach_to()'d to the Agent it is for)
      every tick via _apply_intent() — but keeps its own navigation
      state across ticks: dynamic priority drift and reservations both
      survive from one plan() call to the next.
      The orientation constructor flag (default False) makes plan()
      also fill StepOutcome.orientations, one heading per moving agent
      derived from ORIENTATION_BY_DELTA. Cosmetic only: it does not
      feed back into candidate selection and does not enforce a
      rotate-XOR-move constraint (see the caveat on Agent in
      core/AGENT.md) — an agent can move and face its new direction
      in the same tick.
    end note

    class OneShotGoalDispatcher {
        - _goal_positions : dict[int, Coordinates2D]
        - _priorities     : dict[int, float]
        --
        + assign_missions(agents: list[Agent], grid: Grid2D, step: int) : AssignmentManifest
    }
    note bottom of OneShotGoalDispatcher
      **Scaffolding, not core's FixedGoalDispatcher reborn**
      core's own FixedGoalDispatcher was dropped rather than adapted
      when AssignmentManifest.goals became list[Objective]: it built
      its Objectives at construction time, before any Agent existed to
      attach_to(). This one builds them lazily inside
      assign_missions() instead, where the agents are available — the
      same fixed goal every tick, just enough to drive
      demo_one_shot.py. Not a general-purpose replacement.
    end note

    class LifelongGoalOrchestrator {
        - _target     : dict[int, Coordinates2D | None]
        - _priorities : dict[int, float]
        --
        + set_target(agent_id: int, position: Coordinates2D) : void
        + assign_missions(agents: list[Agent], grid: Grid2D, step: int) : AssignmentManifest
    }
    note bottom of LifelongGoalOrchestrator
      **An externally-targeted counterpart to OneShotGoalDispatcher**
      Where OneShotGoalDispatcher fixes one goal per agent for the
      whole run, this one holds one *mutable* target slot per agent,
      set from outside via set_target() — the caller that owns the
      goal source (an operator click, in sim_dotbot_mrta.py) decides
      what an agent's next target is, not this class. assign_missions()
      only translates current slot state into an AssignmentManifest: an
      agent with a set target emits an Objective; an agent that has
      just reached its target has its slot cleared to None and is
      omitted from goals — it stays parked (like OneShotGoalDispatcher
      already does for an agent with no configured goal) until
      set_target() is called again, which a re-click simply does by
      overwriting the slot. No FCFS, no shared pool: each agent's
      target is independently owned, so nothing needs to arbitrate who
      gets to claim it. Requires PIBTPlanner's
      GoalReachedPolicy.TRANSIENT (the default) — an agent demoted to
      -inf on arrival must be undemoted the instant it is re-targeted,
      or it can never contest a cell again. Drives python -m
      pibt.demo_lifelong (simulated clicks: set_target() called
      directly instead of a real ManualClickTranslator).

      ⚠ **Deliberately no watchdog, no collision check across
      targets.** Nothing stops two agents from being set to the same
      cell — accepted here because rejecting that is an operator-input
      concern (ManualClickTranslator/MRTASession), not this class's
      job: it only reflects requested targets, same allocation/
      navigation split AssignmentManifest already draws. An agent whose
      target turns out unreachable is parked on it forever, same
      accepted limitation as before — the deadlock
      mrta/TaskDrivenOrchestrator's patience/max_attempts used to
      close.
    end note
}

' ── Diamond convention ────────────────────────────────────────────
' Same rule as Class_Diagram/class_diag_commented.puml: filled for an
' immutable value or a mutable object with a single exclusive owner,
' hollow for a shared mutable instance.

PIBTPlanner --|> PathPlanner
OneShotGoalDispatcher --|> MissionOrchestrator
LifelongGoalOrchestrator --|> MissionOrchestrator

' Filled: PIBTPlanner creates and exclusively owns both — nothing else
' references this particular ReservationTable/PriorityManager.
PIBTPlanner "1" *-- "1" ReservationTable : _reservations
PIBTPlanner "1" *-- "1" PriorityManager  : _priorities
' Filled: GoalReachedPolicy is an immutable enum value, same rule as
' Coordinates2D/Orientation elsewhere — sharing the instance is moot.
PIBTPlanner "1" *-- "1" GoalReachedPolicy : _goal_reached_policy

' Dependency, not ownership: run_pibt_pass reads/writes the
' ReservationTable and PriorityManager PIBTPlanner already owns and
' passes in — it never creates or keeps one itself.
PIBTPlanner ..> PibtCore : «calls run_pibt_pass»
PibtCore ..> ReservationTable : «reads/writes»
PibtCore ..> PriorityManager : «reads/writes»

PIBTPlanner ..> Orientation : «emits, if orientation=True»
OneShotGoalDispatcher ..> Objective : «creates, attach_to()'s»
LifelongGoalOrchestrator ..> Objective : «creates, attach_to()'s»

' =====================================================================
'  Page-level legend — the 30-second read.
' =====================================================================
legend bottom left
  **What this diagram covers**
  pibt/: a concrete PathPlanner (Priority Inheritance with
  Backtracking) plus two minimal MissionOrchestrators built on it.
  Depends only on core/ — the same one-directional shape as log/ —
  and knows nothing about any other PathPlanner. OneShotGoalDispatcher
  fixes one goal per agent for the whole run (python -m
  pibt.demo_one_shot, the first thing in the repository to run a full
  tick end to end); LifelongGoalOrchestrator instead holds one
  externally-settable target slot per agent (python -m
  pibt.demo_lifelong) — the regime PIBT already anticipated via
  GoalReachedPolicy, minus the eligibility classes, allocation
  strategy and stall watchdog mrta/TaskDrivenOrchestrator (removed)
  used to add on top; see its own note for the trade-off.
end legend

@enduml
